{
  "configuration_id": "config_002",
  "reason": "Evidence: RNA-only (config_001, acc=0.485) is the dominant signal, image-only is weak (config_010, acc=0.208), and adding image via concat gives a modest boost (config_016, acc=0.527). All three completed experiments use singleprofile, so the effect of multiprofile is entirely untested. Question: Does multiprofile with avgpool aggregation improve RNA representation over singleprofile? Config_002 (rna_only, multiprofile, pca_n2000, avgpool) is a direct, minimal-change comparison to config_001, isolating the profile-setting effect on the dominant modality. Why this is the best next experiment: (1) Multiprofile is the largest unexplored design axis and affects ~2/3 of remaining configs; a negative result here would efficiently prune that branch. (2) Since RNA drives most of the signal, any multiprofile benefit should be most visible in the RNA-only setting, making the result highly interpretable. (3) It avoids conflating the profile-setting change with fusion or image-encoder changes, per the principle of isolating one design decision. The outcome directly informs whether to invest subsequent budget in multiprofile multimodal configurations (configs 018–051) or to focus on encoder/fusion variations within singleprofile."
}

